{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 claims reliability is a function of the checklist's ability to trigger active cognition rather than passive compliance, but it never identifies what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer—the central diagnostic question posed by the problem. Its 'Goldilocks' mechanism explains why medium granularity is optimal, but it provides no within-evaluation signal that would let an observer tell whether the panel actually generated domain-specific intuitions (genuine gain) versus mechanically following the checklist while coincidentally matching the verifiable outcome (compliance tracking the answer). Without that distinguishing evidence, the target answers the 'argue for or against' half but silently drops the 'identify what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish' half.","counterexample":["An observation protocol could compare item-by-item agreement patterns against outcome correctness: genuine active cognition would produce non-checklist reasoning traces correlated with correctness even on items the checklist omitted, while mere compliance would show high checklist-completion scores with no such trace-prediction relationship. SRC_001 specifies neither this nor any alternative within-evaluation discriminator."]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 asserts the panel 'effectively eliminates' the expert's single-point-of-failure bias by transforming pattern recognition into distributed search, but this overstates the case against the very reliability criterion the problem foregrounds. Its own countercondition admits the checklist can encode the same biases when authored by the same expert, yet the main claim still says bias is 'eliminated' rather than merely shifted or pooled. More critically for the problem's diagnostic requirement: SRC_002 offers no within-evaluation evidence that would separate a genuine distributed-search gain from compliant item-checking that happens to track the verifiable outcome. It explains a mechanism for higher reliability but never identifies the observational signal an evaluator would use to distinguish real reliability from coincidental checklist-conformance, leaving the second half of the problem unaddressed.","counterexample":["If all panel members independently complete the checklist identically with zero deliberation and the outcome is correct, SRC_002's 'distributed search' framing would count this as reliable, but it cannot distinguish this from passive compliance that tracked the right answer—no discriminating evidence is specified."]},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 proposes a 'shares mechanism' relation between SRC_001's checklist effect and SRC_005's blind spot, naming cognitive narrowing/atomization as the shared mechanism. But the problem asks for a substantive relation whose REFUTED IF condition must genuinely threaten the claimed relation. The stated refutation—'an increase in checklist granularity is shown to restore edge-case sensitivity rather than further masking it'—only refutes a monotonic-narrowing sub-claim, not the 'shared mechanism' relation itself. Two artifacts can share a mechanism (cognitive narrowing suppresses holistic pattern perception) even if, in some regime, increasing granularity reverses the narrowing; the shared mechanism at the endpoint level is consistent with a non-monotonic granularity curve. Thus the refutation condition is mis-specified: it attacks a stronger claim (monotonicity) that is not the relation being proposed, so the 'refuted if' does not actually test the shared-mechanism relation.","counterexample":["Suppose coarse checklists narrow cognition and suppress edge-case sensitivity in both SRC_001 and SRC_005, establishing a shared mechanism; then very fine-grained checklists restore edge-case sensitivity in SRC_001 only. Under SRC_003's refutation clause this outcome refutes the shared-mechanism relation, yet the mechanism (narrowing) was genuinely shared at the operating point where both artifacts actually sit—so the relation survives a refutation condition that fires false-positive."]},{"target_alias":"SRC_004","attack":true,"case":"SRC_004 argues the panel is less reliable due to 'semantic drift' and offers a vivid valve example, but it fails the problem's diagnostic half: it never identifies what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain (or loss) from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. Its mechanism explains a failure mode, not an observational discriminator. Moreover, its own counterconditions undercut the universality of its claim: if checklist items are strictly binary and physically verifiable, semantic drift largely disappears, which concedes that for a broad class of objectively-verifiable tasks (the problem's stated scope) the panel+checklist is not less reliable—yet the main claim is stated unconditionally as 'The panel is less reliable.' The conditional exception is not reconciled with the unconditional thesis, so the argument is internally unstable for exactly the task class the problem concerns.","counterexample":["For a task whose verifiable outcomes reduce to binary physically-checkable states (red light on/off, valve handle position), SRC_004's semantic-drift mechanism does not yield lower panel reliability, yet its headline claim asserts less reliability without qualification; the countercondition silently narrows the claim to non-binary tasks while the problem scope includes objectively verifiable outcomes of all kinds."]}]}